\section*{Purpose}
This White Sheet describes the system architecture of the Suprakernel Sovereign
Kernel. It formalizes the major subsystems, data flows, and control structures
that implement real-time autonomous kernel evolution with provable stability.

\section{High-Level Architecture}
The architecture is organized into the following core layers:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Sensor Ingestion Gate,
    \item Manifold Projection and Stress Geometry,
    \item Sovereign Execution Core,
    \item Autonomous Kernel Mutation Loop,
    \item Telemetry and Stability Enforcement Layer.
\end{itemize}

\section{Sensor Ingestion Gate}
The ingestion subsystem processes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item FITS/HDFS streams,
    \item cluster telemetry,
    \item state vectors $p(t)$.
\end{itemize}
All inputs are buffered via zero-copy, lock-free ring buffers to maintain
bounded latency and prevent memory corruption.

\section{Riemannian Stress Manifold Layer}
State vectors are mapped to the stress manifold $\mathcal{M}_{\text{stress}}$
using the $\Omega$-mapping operator:
\begin{equation}
\Omega : \mathbb{R}^n \rightarrow \mathcal{M}_{\text{stress}}.
\end{equation}
The Jacobian determinant $J(p)$ is monitored continuously to prevent manifold
inversion and topological tearing.

\section{Sovereign Execution Core}
The sovereign core coordinates:
\begin{itemize}
    \item CUDA/PTX kernel execution,
    \item distributed state updates,
    \item manifold-aware scheduling,
    \item stability-aware resource allocation.
\end{itemize}
Execution is deployed across a 64-node NVIDIA HGX H100 cluster with synchronized
CUDA streams and NVLink interconnects.

\section{Autonomous Kernel Mutation Loop}
Runtime evolution is governed by the four-stage loop:
\[
\text{Observe} \rightarrow \text{Orient} \rightarrow \text{Mutate} \rightarrow \text{Verify}.
\]
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Observe:} Ingest $p(t)$ and telemetry $\mathcal{T}(t)$.
    \item \textbf{Orient:} Project onto $\mathcal{M}_{\text{stress}}$, compute $J(p)$ and Lyapunov margin.
    \item \textbf{Mutate:} Propose kernel updates $\Delta K$ subject to
    \[
    \|\Delta K\| \le \kappa_{\max}(t).
    \]
    \item \textbf{Verify:} Enforce
    \[
    \dot{L}(p) < 0,\quad J(p) > 0,\quad
    \dot{h}(p) + \gamma_{\text{CBF}} h(p) \ge 0.
    \]
\end{itemize}

\section{Telemetry and Stability Enforcement}
A sovereign telemetry daemon streams:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{T}(t) = \{ J(p), \alpha(t), \beta(t), U(p), B_{\text{mem}}, U_{\text{tensor}} \},
\end{equation}
into the core. These signals drive:
\begin{itemize}
    \item adaptive damping $\mathbf{D}(p)$,
    \item mutation amplitude bounds $\kappa_{\max}(t)$,
    \item CBF enforcement via real-time QP,
    \item manifold curvature corrections.
\end{itemize}

\section{Cluster-Level Coordination}
Across all nodes, the architecture maintains:
\begin{equation}
\dot{L}(p) < 0,\qquad
\langle \mathbf{D}(p), p \rangle \ge c_d \|p\|^2,\qquad
J(p) > 0.
\end{equation}
Distributed MPI/RDMA channels synchronize:
\begin{itemize}
    \item local manifold states,
    \item Lyapunov margins,
    \item mutation decisions,
    \item telemetry aggregates.
\end{itemize}

\section{Architectural Guarantees}
By design, the architecture prohibits:
\begin{itemize}
    \item numerical divergence,
    \item manifold inversion,
    \item topological rupture,
    \item unstable kernel evolution.
\end{itemize}

